          %h3#spaces_of_homomorphisms Spaces of homomorphisms
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nearby+homomorphisms+from+compact+Lie+groups+are+conjugate"} nearby homomorphisms from compact Lie groups are conjugate
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+group"} discrete group
                is a compact Lie group iff it is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+group"} finite group
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+Lie+groups"} classical Lie groups
                for
                %em definite
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product+space"} inner products
                are compact, such as the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+groups"} orthogonal groups
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+groups"} unitary groups
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternionic+unitary+groups"} quaternionic unitary groups
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+group"} Lorentz group
                etc.
          %h2#applications Applications
          %h3#InEquivariantHomotopyTheory In equivariant homotopy theory
          %p
            Compact Lie groups make a somewhat unexpected appearance as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance+groups"} equivariance groups
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+theory"} equivariant homotopy theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariance+group"} equivariance group
            is needed in order for (the available proofs of) the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+Whitehead+theorem"} equivariant Whitehead theorem
            to hold.
          %p
            (Namely, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+triangulation+theorem"} equivariant triangulation theorem
            %a{:href => "#EquivariantTriangulationTheorem"} above
            is used in these proofs to guaratee that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+products"} Cartesian products
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coset+spaces"} coset spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G/H
            are themselves
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-CW-complexes"} G-CW-complexes
          %h3#in_gauge_theory In gauge theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"}> Yang-Mills theory
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"}> Chern-Simons theory
            , …) …
